    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is largely coherent and additive \u2014 putting `fiscalYear(LocalDate)` on `RegistrationDateNormalizer` sits naturally next to the existing date-derivation logic (the class javadoc already frames itself as the home for \"any value derived from the registration date\"), and the three call sites reuse it cleanly. Two structural nits, though:\n\n1. **Misplaced field in `RegistrationDateNormalizer` (lines 64\u201365).** The new `FISCAL_YEAR_START_MONTH` constant is declared at the *bottom* of the class, after the `normalize`/`isWeekend` methods, whereas the class's other constant (`PUBLIC_HOLIDAYS`) lives at the top before the private constructor. A maintainer of this file would expect all static fields grouped together at the top. Move `FISCAL_YEAR_START_MONTH` up next to `PUBLIC_HOLIDAYS` so the class keeps its fields-then-constructor-then-methods ordering.\n\n2. **Duplicated two-digit fiscal-year formatting in `OwnerMapper`.** Both `membershipNumber` (line 117\u2013118) and `fiscalYear` (line 132\u2013133) independently do `RegistrationDateNormalizer.fiscalYear(...)` followed by `String.format(\"%02d\", fiscalYear % 100)`. The \"last two digits of the fiscal year\" is now a shared concept expressed twice. Consider a small private helper (e.g. `fiscalYearYY(LocalDate)` returning the `%02d`-formatted two digits) that both methods call, so the derivation lives in one place. This is minor given the mapper's convention of self-contained default methods, but the repetition is real now that two fields depend on the same segment.\n\nNeither point affects correctness; they're about keeping the units tidy and the derivation single-sourced.",
